james wrote:

	      [about pudding farming]

> How necessary is the healing spell?

Not necessary at all.

If you're in an altar-containing room with a hundred
puddings crowding around, and an AC good enough that
they can't bite you, or naked if they are brown
puddings and you have a good naked AD, essentially
you kill off some of the HP:1 puddings while the
others heal to the point of being able to divide when
struck with an IDed iron knife or dagger dulled to
-3 enchantment by repeated use to engrave the floor.

You don't need a healing spell, just patience and a
stethoscope, so that you can you leave the high HP
puddings for last after eliminating the weak ones to
make room for the others to split.

If there are no high HP puddings, wait a bit; they
heal without your help, just adding HPs slowly one
by one but rapidly as a mass if you keep enough of
them alive.

My last attempt at pudding farming, they were
spilling out of the temple (Mines Town) to fill most
of the whole level, which created a "door
management" problem: the ones I wanted to split were
the older ones, who were mostly outside trying to
get closer, but I couldn't afford to go outside, for
fear of being cut off from the altar.

So, I stood in the doorway to hit the high HP ones
outside in the hall.

Some of the offspring ended up in the room behind
me, and I kept going until it was full again.

[The placement algorithm seems to find the nearest
empty spot for them, even on the other side of
solid rock.]

I also had to push boulders to fill in temple wall
gaps as various misfortunes made holes there, so I
could limit the other monsters from sneaking in
behind my PC's back. The boulders don't dissuade
the puddings, who will slide under a door or a
boulder if nothing better offers itself as a
passageway.

If I had it to do it all again, I'd knock down all
the walls (except the shop walls, if in Mines Town
instead of at some unattended altar) before
starting, to make the puddings self managing and the
crowding in by the healthier ones easier for them,
and to give my PC a better warning line of sight to
spot other arriving monsters earlier.

Other pudding farmers have mentioned chopping out
all the rock for an entire level before starting, as
an extension to the same concept; I've done that
before, but not as a pudding farming tactic, just to
have done it one time for fun.

> One of the P's ought to drop the spellbook
> evenutally, right?

Yes, but accumulation of death drop items, while as
steady as a random process gets, is also agonizingly
slow. You don't talk about what non-useless stuff
you expect in tens of pudding kills, but in tens of
thousands of pudding kills.

If you are altar squatting, though, and manage your
luck and alignment in the ways others have
documented to avoid getting crowned, you should be
on the receiving end of a steady supply of
spellbooks from your deity, which will speed things
up in terms of getting one you especially want. Pay
attention that you need to read them as you go, to
keep from getting duplicates from your deity, so you
need a huge stash of starting holy water, or some
nearby pools that let you dilute other potions
regularly (you'll get _lots_ of potions as death
drops).

> I realize I'm contemplating a quarter-million-turn
> game, the sort that puts hundreds of pages of
> inventory on your altar :-)

Boudewijn and you are going to be future best
buddies, I suspect.

Really good things to have for this process are
bags, boxes, and chests to pile on the altar.

Named by their contents type, they let you organize
your stash in ways to preserve your sanity.

You can sort stuff by kind, by BUC status, by
eventual use, or whatever seems best to you.

You most especially want a separate trash container,
where you put stuff you never expect to touch except
possibly as polypile fodder, so you don't have to
wade through it on lists every time you look for the
good stuff.

You'll find yourself _constantly_ rearranging stuff,
as receipt of extra-goodies makes former goodies
lose luster and rate demotion.

Your trash container will soon be the heaviest
container (and you never expect to lift it intact
with its contents), so put it on top of the stack,
to discourage pets and wandering large monsters from
messing with your stashes, and don't lift other
containers out from under it until you are done
farming, since you won't be able to move it back
to the top without a lot of wasted time to empty it,
life it, and refill it.

Always leave one cursed item, and if you have one, a
scroll of scare monster, on the altar outside any
container, for similar pet and wandering monster
dissuading reasons, and as a retreat.

